      { twinId: 2, inputText: 'How should I hedge a concentrated stock position?', expectedOutput: 'Several strategies exist for hedging concentrated stock positions, each with different risk-return and tax implications. Protective puts provide downside protection while maintaining upside but have ongoing premium costs. Costless collars (buying puts funded by selling covered calls) eliminate premium cost but cap upside. Prepaid variable forwards provide immediate liquidity while deferring capital gains but are complex and may face IRS scrutiny. Exchange funds allow diversification by pooling concentrated positions with other investors, deferring gains. For tax-loss harvesting, direct indexing of a broad portfolio can generate losses to offset gains from eventual position reduction. The optimal approach depends on your cost basis, investment horizon, liquidity needs, and risk tolerance.', category: 'investment', quality: 0.92, verified: true, usageCount: 8 },
      { twinId: 3, inputText: 'When should I use GraphQL instead of REST?', expectedOutput: 'Choose GraphQL when: your frontend needs flexible data fetching across multiple related entities (reducing over-fetching and under-fetching), you have multiple client types (web, mobile, IoT) with different data requirements from the same API, or your data model has deep, interconnected relationships. Stick with REST when: you need simple CRUD operations with well-defined resource boundaries, HTTP caching is critical for performance (REST leverages HTTP caching natively), you want simpler server-side implementation with established tooling, or your API serves primarily server-to-server communication where payload flexibility is less important. Consider a hybrid approach: REST for simple resource endpoints and GraphQL for complex, relationship-heavy queries. Be aware that GraphQL introduces complexity in areas like rate limiting, authorization at the field level, and N+1 query problems that require DataLoader patterns.', category: 'technical', quality: 0.93, verified: true, usageCount: 15 },
      { twinId: 4, inputText: 'How do I design an effective rubric for project-based assessment?', expectedOutput: 'Effective project rubrics should have 4-6 performance dimensions, each with 3-5 clearly defined achievement levels. Start by identifying the learning objectives the project addresses and map each to a rubric dimension. Use concrete, observable behavioral descriptors rather than vague qualifiers -- instead of "excellent understanding," write "correctly applies three or more course concepts to analyze the problem and provides evidence-based justification." Include both process and product criteria: research quality, analytical depth, communication clarity, creativity, and technical execution. Provide exemplars at each performance level so students can self-assess. Weight dimensions according to learning priorities. For formative assessment, include a self-assessment component where students rate themselves before submission. Share the rubric at project assignment, not just at grading, to set clear expectations and guide student effort.', category: 'assessment', quality: 0.9, verified: true, usageCount: 7 },
      { twinId: 5, inputText: 'What is the ideal posting frequency for LinkedIn B2B marketing?', expectedOutput: 'For B2B LinkedIn marketing, data suggests optimal posting frequency varies by content type and company size. For company pages: 3-5 posts per week provides consistent visibility without audience fatigue. For executive thought leadership (personal profiles): 2-3 posts per week generates optimal engagement, as LinkedIn algorithm favors personal content. For each post type, optimal timing varies: industry insights perform best Tuesday-Thursday 7-8am, case studies perform best midweek during lunch hours, and engagement-style posts (polls, questions) work well on Monday and Friday. Key principle: consistency matters more than frequency. A reliable 3x/week schedule outperforms inconsistent daily posting. Each post should provide standalone value -- avoid multi-part series that require sequential consumption. Use a 4-1-1 content ratio: 4 pieces of curated or educational content, 1 piece of original thought leadership, and 1 piece of promotional content.', category: 'social media', quality: 0.88, verified: true, usageCount: 9 },
      { twinId: 6, inputText: 'What is the difference between copyright and patent protection for software?', expectedOutput: 'Copyright and patents protect different aspects of software. Copyright protects the specific expression of code -- the literal source code, object code, and the structure, sequence, and organization of the program. It arises automatically upon creation, lasts for the author life plus 70 years (or 95 years for works for hire), and prevents literal copying and substantially similar reproductions. Patents protect the functional innovation -- the novel method, process, or system that the software implements. Patents require filing, examination, and approval (12-36 months typical), last 20 years from filing date, and provide stronger protection against independent creation but are more expensive to obtain and enforce. For comprehensive software protection, pursue both: copyright registration for the codebase (inexpensive, fast) and patent protection for genuinely novel algorithms or technical solutions (expensive but broader protection). Trade secret protection can supplement both for proprietary implementations you do not disclose.', category: 'intellectual property', quality: 0.94, verified: true, usageCount: 6 },
      { twinId: 7, inputText: 'How do I calculate my company Scope 2 emissions?', expectedOutput: 'Scope 2 emissions are indirect emissions from purchased electricity, steam, heating, and cooling. Calculate using two methods as required by the GHG Protocol Scope 2 Guidance. Location-based method: multiply your electricity consumption (kWh) by the grid emission factor for your geographic location (available from EPA eGRID for the US, or national grid emission factors from IEA). Market-based method: uses emission factors from your specific energy procurement choices -- contractual instruments like Renewable Energy Certificates (RECs), Power Purchase Agreements (PPAs), or supplier-specific emission factors. If you purchase 100% renewable energy with matching RECs, your market-based Scope 2 can be zero. Gather data from utility bills (12 months minimum), apply appropriate conversion factors for each facility location, and sum across all locations. Report both methods in your GHG inventory. For steam and heating, use supplier-provided emission factors or default factors from the GHG Protocol guidance.', category: 'emissions', quality: 0.91, verified: true, usageCount: 5 },
      { twinId: 8, inputText: 'What KPIs should I track for warehouse operations?', expectedOutput: 'Essential warehouse KPIs fall into five categories. Receiving: dock-to-stock time (target: <24 hours), receiving accuracy rate (target: >99.5%), and put-away productivity (units per labor hour). Storage: inventory accuracy (target: >99.7% by location), space utilization rate (target: 85-90%), and inventory carrying cost as a percentage of inventory value. Picking: pick accuracy (target: >99.9%), picks per labor hour, and order cycle time from release to pack-complete. Shipping: on-time shipment rate (target: >99%), perfect order rate (on-time, complete, undamaged, accurate documentation), and cost per order shipped. Overall: inventory turnover rate (industry-specific benchmarks), total warehouse cost per unit, and labor productivity across all functions. Track these daily with rolling weekly and monthly trends. Use real-time dashboards for picking and shipping metrics and weekly reviews for strategic metrics.', category: 'operations', quality: 0.9, verified: true, usageCount: 11 },
      { twinId: 9, inputText: 'What are the signs of burnout versus normal work stress?', expectedOutput: 'While stress and burnout share some symptoms, they differ in key ways. Work stress is typically acute and situational -- you feel overwhelmed but still engaged, and symptoms resolve when the stressor is removed. You might feel urgency and hyperactivity. Burnout, as defined by the Maslach model, involves three distinct dimensions: emotional exhaustion (feeling drained and unable to recover even with rest), depersonalization or cynicism (detachment from work, colleagues, and clients, often manifested as sarcasm or indifference), and reduced personal accomplishment (feeling ineffective and doubting the value of your work). Burnout develops gradually over months. Physical signs include chronic fatigue unrelieved by rest, frequent illness from immune suppression, sleep disruption patterns, and changes in appetite. Behavioral signs include withdrawal from responsibilities, procrastination on previously manageable tasks, and decreased productivity despite longer hours. If three or more of these patterns persist for more than two weeks, a professional assessment is recommended.', category: 'mental health', quality: 0.93, verified: true, usageCount: 14 },
      { twinId: 10, inputText: 'How should I respond to a ransomware attack?', expectedOutput: 'Ransomware incident response follows a critical sequence. Immediate (first 30 minutes): isolate affected systems by disconnecting from the network (do NOT power off as this may destroy forensic evidence), activate your incident response team and plan, and notify executive leadership. Assessment (hours 1-4): determine the ransomware variant and scope of encryption, identify patient zero and attack vector, assess backup integrity and availability, and preserve forensic evidence including ransom notes, encrypted file samples, and system logs. Containment (hours 4-24): ensure the attacker no longer has active access, patch the vulnerability used for initial access, and reset all compromised credentials. Recovery: restore from verified clean backups, rebuild compromised systems from known-good images, and implement additional monitoring. Communication: notify legal counsel, determine regulatory notification obligations (GDPR: 72 hours), consider law enforcement engagement (FBI IC3), and prepare stakeholder communications. Critical: do NOT pay the ransom without consulting legal counsel and law enforcement -- payment does not guarantee decryption and may violate OFAC sanctions regulations.', category: 'incident response', quality: 0.95, verified: true, usageCount: 10 },
      { twinId: 11, inputText: 'How do I write a good product requirements document?', expectedOutput: 'A strong PRD should contain these sections: Problem Statement (what user pain point are we solving, backed by data -- support tickets, user research, market analysis), User Personas (who specifically will use this feature, with behavioral context), Success Metrics (how will we measure if this feature is successful -- specific, measurable targets), User Stories and Requirements (functional requirements as user stories with acceptance criteria, plus non-functional requirements for performance, security, and accessibility), Scope Definition (explicit in-scope and out-of-scope boundaries to prevent scope creep), Design and UX Requirements (wireframes or mockups, interaction flows, edge cases), Technical Considerations (API requirements, data model changes, third-party integrations, migration needs), Dependencies and Risks (cross-team dependencies, technical risks, timeline risks with mitigation plans), and Timeline (milestones with dates, not just a delivery date). Keep it concise -- 3-5 pages maximum. The PRD should be a living document updated as decisions are made during development.', category: 'product management', quality: 0.91, verified: true, usageCount: 13 },
      { twinId: 12, inputText: 'How do I evaluate a triple-net lease investment?', expectedOutput: 'Triple-net (NNN) lease evaluation focuses on four pillars. Tenant credit quality: analyze financial statements, credit ratings (investment grade preferred), industry outlook, and store-level sales performance (for retail). The tenant essentially IS the investment in NNN. Lease terms: remaining lease term (15+ years preferred for acquisition), rental escalation structure (fixed bumps of 1-2% annually, CPI-linked, or percentage rent), renewal options, and termination clauses. Property fundamentals: location quality (traffic counts, demographics, accessibility), building condition and remaining useful life, environmental status, and highest-and-best-use analysis if tenant vacates. Financial analysis: going-in cap rate versus market (currently 5.5-7% depending on tenant credit), implied price per square foot versus replacement cost, and cash-on-cash return after debt service. Red flags: single-tenant concentration risk, below-market rent (creates replacement tenant risk at market rents), short remaining lease term without renewal certainty, and markets with declining population or employment trends.', category: 'real estate investment', quality: 0.92, verified: true, usageCount: 7 },
      { twinId: 13, inputText: 'How do I handle class imbalance in a classification model?', expectedOutput: 'Class imbalance requires a multi-faceted approach. Data-level techniques: SMOTE (Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique) generates synthetic minority class examples by interpolating between existing samples -- use SMOTE variants like ADASYN for adaptive synthesis in borderline regions. Random under-sampling of the majority class works well when you have abundant data. Algorithm-level techniques: cost-sensitive learning assigns higher misclassification costs to the minority class (use class_weight parameter in scikit-learn). Ensemble methods like BalancedRandomForest or EasyEnsemble combine sampling with bagging for robust performance. Evaluation adjustments: never use accuracy as the primary metric for imbalanced classes. Use precision-recall AUC (PR-AUC), F1 score, and Matthews Correlation Coefficient (MCC). Calibrate probability outputs using Platt scaling or isotonic regression. Threshold optimization: the default 0.5 decision threshold is rarely optimal for imbalanced data. Use precision-recall curves to select the threshold that best balances business objectives. In practice, combining SMOTE on the training set with cost-sensitive learning and threshold tuning provides the most robust results.', category: 'machine learning', quality: 0.94, verified: true, usageCount: 16 },
      { twinId: 14, inputText: 'What documents are needed for customs clearance when exporting to the EU?', expectedOutput: 'EU customs clearance requires several mandatory documents. Commercial Invoice: must include complete buyer/seller details, HS/CN commodity codes, country of origin, Incoterms, currency, and item-level values. Packing List: itemized contents with weights, dimensions, and package markings. Bill of Lading or Airway Bill: transport document serving as receipt and contract of carriage. Certificate of Origin: may be required for preferential tariff treatment under FTAs -- use EUR.1 movement certificate or origin declaration for authorized exporters. Customs Declaration (Single Administrative Document, SAD): electronic submission through the EU Import Control System. Additionally: EORI number registration is mandatory for all EU importers, CE marking documentation for applicable product categories, REACH compliance declarations for chemicals, phytosanitary certificates for agricultural products, and conformity assessment documentation for regulated products. For CBAM-covered goods, carbon content declarations will be required. Work with a licensed customs broker for complex shipments and maintain documentation for 7 years minimum for audit purposes.', category: 'trade compliance', quality: 0.9, verified: true, usageCount: 8 },
      { twinId: 15, inputText: 'How do I create an effective employee onboarding program?', expectedOutput: 'Effective onboarding extends well beyond the first day and should span the first 90 days minimum. Pre-boarding (before Day 1): send welcome materials, complete IT equipment provisioning, assign a buddy/mentor, share first-week schedule, and provide access to self-paced pre-reading materials. Week 1: focus on belonging -- team introductions, company culture immersion, workspace setup, essential tool training, and one-on-one with direct manager to align on 30-60-90 day expectations. Month 1: role-specific training, cross-functional introductions, shadow sessions with key stakeholders, first meaningful work assignment with achievable scope, and weekly manager check-ins. Months 2-3: increasing responsibility, mid-probation feedback session, participation in team planning, and connection to broader company initiatives. Key metrics to track: time-to-productivity (when the new hire is contributing independently), new hire satisfaction (survey at 30, 60, and 90 days), new hire retention (% still employed at 12 months), and manager satisfaction with new hire readiness. Companies with structured onboarding achieve 58% higher 3-year retention rates.', category: 'talent management', quality: 0.91, verified: true, usageCount: 9 },
      { twinId: 5, inputText: 'How do I calculate Customer Acquisition Cost for a multi-channel campaign?', expectedOutput: 'Customer Acquisition Cost (CAC) for multi-channel campaigns requires careful attribution modeling. Basic formula: Total Marketing and Sales Spend divided by Number of New Customers Acquired in the period. For multi-channel accuracy, break this down by channel: allocate direct costs (ad spend, content creation, tool subscriptions) to each channel, then distribute shared costs (marketing team salaries, overhead) proportionally based on time allocation or effort. For attribution: first-touch attribution credits the awareness channel, last-touch credits the converting channel, and multi-touch models (linear, time-decay, position-based) distribute credit across all touchpoints. Use UTM parameters and CRM integration to track the full customer journey. Calculate both blended CAC (total spend / total customers) and channel-specific CAC. The critical metric is the CAC-to-LTV ratio: target a ratio below 1:3 (each dollar of acquisition cost returns three dollars in lifetime value). Include a CAC payback period calculation to assess cash flow implications.', category: 'marketing analytics', quality: 0.89, verified: true, usageCount: 11 }
